==Danish==

===Etymology===
Earlier also written {{m|da|herr}}, a short form of {{m|da|herre||gentleman, mister, master}}. Titles tend to be shortened in a pretonic position in Danish, compare {{m|da|frue}} > {{m|da|fru||Mrs.}}, {{m|da|konge}} > {{m|da|kong||King ...}}, {{m|da|greve}} > {{m|da|grev||Count ...}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|da|/hɛr/|[ˈhaɐ̯]}}

===Noun===
{{head|da|noun|uninflected}}

# {{lb|da|title}} [[mister]], [[Mr.]]

====Usage notes====
* Used in direct address ([[vocative]]) or prefixed with a personal name, e.g. ''Kom indenfor, hr.'', ''Jeg mødte hr. Jensen i går''.
* In the present Danish language, it is unusual to refer to another person with a title and the last name, even in formal contexts.

